Raw dev notes -- Taming the AI Beast: From Stale Hosts to Streaming Success

This past week felt like a whirlwind of both deep-dive fixes and exciting new features for the AI agent platform. The project, let's call it Weaverai, is all about building intelligent agents that can process documents, automate workflows, and interact with users through chat. It's our internal platform for making AI capabilities accessible and robust for various tasks, from translation to data extraction.

What got done this period was a real mix. On the feature front, we pushed out some significant user experience improvements. The chat UI now has a much-needed per-code-block copy button, and we generally cleaned up text/rich message copying across the board, along with a button restyle for the tools UI. A big win was getting per-user job tracking for things like translate and anonymize, consolidating it into a unified "My Jobs" section in the Tools-UI. We also added opt-in email and browser notifications for when transcription jobs are done, which should be a nice quality-of-life improvement.

Behind the scenes, we made some critical infrastructure and security moves. We de-embedded a sensitive API key via an internal authenticated proxy workflow, which is a big step for security. We also started on-prem-assisted memory consolidation, which sounds complex but is crucial for performance and data handling. Ops-wise, we rolled out standard failure notifiers for all our n8n workflows and even added a fourth ops watcher for n8n service health. Oh, and the weekly commit digest as a pod + n8n Graph email is a neat way to keep everyone in the loop.

But the real highlight, and frankly, a huge relief, was getting the streaming POC to a "GREENLIGHT verdict." This means we can start delivering responses to users much faster, which is a game-changer for perceived performance. We even added a staged progress ticker in the chat UI to show users that things are happening while they wait.

Of course, it wasn't all smooth sailing. There were a bunch of annoying fixes. The n8n gateway front-door broke due to stale hostnames – classic infra headache. Word uploads were failing with HTTP 400s, which turned out to be a routing issue that needed to go through Docling. Pasted text in QMS chat was causing 500 errors because of control characters, so we had to strip those out before sending to Flowise. And the xlsx bake-off readers were broken, which meant fixing four of them and rebuilding the runner. We also had to stop tracking n8n workflow backups because they were leaking user document payloads – a good catch on the security/privacy front. The QMS chat history also got some love, moving to an OWUI-backed system, and we fixed a phantom progress bar issue for stale pending jobs.

Honestly, this week felt like a constant battle against the little gremlins in the system, interspersed with moments of genuine progress. Getting streaming to work was a huge morale boost, especially after wrestling with so many small, fiddly bugs. It's a reminder that building robust AI systems isn't just about the fancy models; it's about the plumbing, the security, the user experience, and the endless pursuit of stability. It felt good to make the platform more resilient and user-friendly, even if it meant diving into some gnarly issues. The constant merging of branches also highlighted how much parallel work is happening, which is exciting but also means more careful coordination.

Next up: Diving deeper into streaming implementation and refining our document processing capabilities.
